  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none",
    "relation": "discusses",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study measures within-network feature–classifier alignment and attention-head overlap, but does not test whether representations from independently trained models become more similar with scale or capability. Its head-overlap results are related to, but distinct from, the claim’s cross-model representation convergence.",
    "gate_note": "No cross-model representation-distance comparison, paired image-caption alignment test, or scaling-versus-capability test is reported. The closest results are query-subspace overlap among heads within individual Transformers, compared with random-subspace baselines. Other experiments concern within-network classifier–feature alignment and activation-induced basis obstruction.",
    "test_reason": "The study measures within-network alignment (feature–classifier subspace alignment in MLPs/ResNets/ViT, commutator obstruction, and query-head subspace overlap within single pretrained Transformers). It never compares representations across independently trained models, seeds, modalities or scales, so no result bears on any version. It mentions CKA/SVCCA and argues subspace metrics are 'mathematical necessities', but this is a theoretical remark on metric choice, not a test of measured cross-model convergence (V6).",
    "authors_conclusion": "The record's paraphrase is accurate. The authors argue subspace incidence (intersection dimension) is the unique reparameterization-invariant alignment observable. Ridge regularization drives Level-2 feature–classifier alignment, activation anisotropy obstructs Level-3 basis alignment, and head-subspace overlap is an exploratory, weight-only diagnostic of head redundancy.",
    "recipe": "none",
    "recipe_differences": "The study does not implement or compare the original cross-model representation-similarity method; it measures within-network subspace relations using Frobenius overlap and other alignment diagnostics.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported head-overlap values vary by layer and model. GPT-2 small has an increasing depth profile overall, while GPT-2 medium has lower mean overlap than GPT-2 small despite being larger. The study does not compare alternative similarity metrics or input datasets.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Query-head subspaces within pretrained GPT-2 small, DistilGPT-2, GPT-2 medium and Llama-3.2-1B overlap more than Haar-random baselines in every layer. Random-init GPT-2 matches the baseline, and GPT-2 medium overlaps less than GPT-2 small.",
        "proposed_claim": "Within a trained Transformer, attention-head query subspaces overlap more than random subspaces (head redundancy); this is not monotone in model size.",
